---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { DASHBOARD_DATA_TODOS } from '../data/dashboardMetrics';
---
<BaseLayout title="Methodology · Game Industry Observatory">
  <section class="hero compact">
    <p class="eyebrow">Transparency</p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">Source figures, short updates and visible limits. No combined industry score.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="prose panel">
    <h2>Scope and dates</h2>
    <p>Each figure keeps its source, population, region, period and type. Countries, platforms and company results remain separate. Annual benchmarks keep their original dates when carried forward.</p>

    <h2>Data types</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reported:</strong> published or counted by the source.</li>
      <li><strong>Survey:</strong> applies to respondents or participating institutions.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculated / Derived:</strong> arithmetic with stored inputs and a formula.</li>
      <li><strong>Forecast:</strong> a projection.</li>
      <li><strong>Estimate:</strong> a modelled figure.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reading the dashboard</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Markets:</strong> global forecasts, US players and European revenue use different definitions and periods.</li>
      <li><strong>Jobs:</strong> advertised vacancies measure demand, not completed hires. A removed listing is not proof of hiring.</li>
      <li><strong>Layoffs:</strong> trackers count documented announcements; incomplete disclosures can undercount workers.</li>
      <li><strong>Employer exposure:</strong> respondents reporting employer layoffs, not a percentage of companies.</li>
      <li><strong>Graduate entry:</strong> an unweighted median of selected undergraduate programme rates. Missing rates and postgraduate programmes are excluded.</li>
      <li><strong>Career switching:</strong> the source combines changes of field and industry; its question response count is undisclosed.</li>
      <li><strong>Joblessness:</strong> outcomes among laid-off survey respondents, not a worldwide available-worker count.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Education and worker movement</h2>
    <p>Game-specific education requires an explicitly games-focused programme or classification. Broader computing, animation and creative courses remain adjacent. Awards count credentials, not unique people or new workers.</p>
    <p>Employer separations, moves between games companies, industry exits, non-employment and intent to leave are distinct. Survey percentages are never multiplied by tracked layoffs to estimate a global workforce surplus.</p>
    <p>The <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}statistical-sources/`}>statistical registry</a> lists datasets and their coverage limits.</p>

    <h3>Open measurement work</h3>
    <ul>
      {DASHBOARD_DATA_TODOS.map(item => <li><strong>{item.label}:</strong> {item.evidenceNeeded} {item.nextStep}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Evidence and independence</h2>
    <p>Reports repeating one source count as one origin. Known ownership, funding and source relationships are recorded; unknown relationships stay unknown. Company registers count legal entities, not necessarily studios.</p>
    <p>Evidence records retain source URLs, dataset IDs, dates, revisions, classifications, filters and transformations. Raw files are retained where practical; large or restricted files can use release IDs, external references and hashes.</p>

    <h2>Writing and review</h2>
    <p>Reports lead with a brief update, then key findings and data. AI may summarise evidence, including non-English sources, but must preserve its meaning and uncertainty. Missing values stay missing.</p>
    <p>Every report states its review status. Human review is not an independent audit. Coverage favours English-language, North American and European sources; private companies and smaller studios are less visible.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
